"""FILE: vidbyte/tools/builtins/codex/descriptions.py

PURPOSE: Holds the model-facing text of the twenty-six CodexHost tools: one description per tool and one per parameter, each written as general sentences the model reads at call time.
ROLE IN CODEBASE: Imported by the tool modules of vidbyte/tools/builtins/codex/, which put these strings into each ToolSpec and its input schema.
ARCHITECTURE NOTE: Plain string constants with no imports, so the text can change without touching tool behaviour. Each string is one literal, never implicitly concatenated, and has at least four sentences with no concrete examples.
COMMON MODIFICATION PATTERNS: A new parameter adds one constant here and one schema property in its tool; a WRITE tool's description always says it starts or changes paid Codex work.
KNOWN EDGE CASES: Constant names avoid the words the security lint reads as credentials, so the goal budgets' descriptions are SPAWN_GOAL_BUDGET_DESCRIPTION and RUN_GOAL_BUDGET_DESCRIPTION.
RELATED DOCS: docs/spec/codex-host/spec.md (section 9.2 tool table); docs/spec/codex-host-thread-helpers/spec.md (section 9.2 tool table and description rules)
TESTS: tests/features/codex_host/test_codex_host_tools.py, tests/features/codex_host/test_codex_host_helper_tools.py
"""

from __future__ import annotations

CHECK_CODEX_READY_DESCRIPTION = "Checks whether the Codex runtime behind this host is ready to run turns, without starting any work. It reports the runtime version against the minimum version the host needs, whether an account is signed in, the account kind and plan, and on Windows whether the Codex sandbox is set up. Every problem it finds is listed in the result together with the next step that fixes it, rather than being raised as a failure. Call it before starting work when a previous call failed for a setup reason, or when you need to know whether Codex can run at all."
GET_CODEX_RATE_LIMITS_DESCRIPTION = "Reads the usage windows of the Codex account behind this host, without starting any work. The result says whether ordinary usage is allowed right now, which plan the account is on, which limit was reached if any, and how full the primary and secondary windows are and when they reset. A missing allowance is reported as unknown rather than guessed. Call it when a turn failed because usage was limited, or before starting a large batch of work."
LIST_CODEX_MODELS_DESCRIPTION = "Lists the models the Codex runtime behind this host offers, reading every page of the list. Each entry gives the model id and name, a display name and description, whether it is hidden or the default, its default reasoning effort and the efforts it supports. Use the id or name of a listed model whenever another tool takes a model, because an unlisted name is refused before any work starts. Hidden models are left out unless you ask for them."
SPAWN_CODEX_THREAD_DESCRIPTION = "Starts a new Codex thread and its first turn, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running after this call returns. Give it either a prompt for one turn or a goal objective that Codex keeps working toward across turns until the goal ends. The call returns a handle as soon as Codex accepts the work, and you collect the outcome later with the wait or result tool. The output token cap is enforced by Vidbyte, which interrupts the turn once its own output passes the cap, because Codex has no such limit of its own. The sandbox, approvals, working folder and instructions come from the developer's settings and cannot be changed here."
CONTINUE_CODEX_THREAD_DESCRIPTION = "Starts one more turn on an existing Codex thread, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running after this call returns. The thread keeps its history, so the new prompt can build on everything said before, and any thread id this host can see is accepted. The call is refused when the thread already has a running turn, so steer or wait on that turn instead. It returns a handle as soon as Codex accepts the turn, and you collect the outcome later with the wait or result tool."
FORK_CODEX_THREAD_DESCRIPTION = "Copies an existing Codex thread into a new thread through one of its completed turns, which changes the stored Codex threads of the account. The new thread holds every turn up to the fork point and can then be continued on its own without touching the original. The fork point must be a completed turn, and when none is given the last completed turn is used. The result describes the new thread, including its id, which the continue tool takes."
LIST_CODEX_THREADS_DESCRIPTION = "Lists one page of the stored Codex threads of the account, newest according to the chosen sort. Each entry describes a thread by its id, name, preview, working folder, status, model and timestamps. Without source filters Codex lists only threads started interactively, so ask for the app server source to see threads started by tools like these. Pass the returned cursor to read the next page, which is empty when there are no more."
READ_CODEX_THREAD_DESCRIPTION = "Reads one stored Codex thread by its id, without starting any work. The result describes the thread by its name, preview, working folder, status, model and timestamps, and can include a summary of each of its turns. A turn summary gives the turn's status, its final answer and any error message. Use it to look at a thread before continuing or forking it, or to read the final answers of turns that already ended."
WAIT_CODEX_TURN_DESCRIPTION = "Waits for a running Codex turn that this toolset started, for at most a bounded number of seconds. If the turn ends in time the result is its final snapshot, with its status, final answer, usage and any problem; otherwise the result says the turn is still running. Waiting never stops or changes the turn, so you can wait again as often as you need. The wait is capped at a few minutes per call so that a long turn never blocks the caller for too long."
GET_CODEX_TURN_RESULT_DESCRIPTION = "Returns the current snapshot of a Codex turn that this toolset started, without waiting. A turn that is still running is reported as running, and a turn that ended carries its status, final answer, usage and any problem with its next step. Reading a result never contacts Codex and never changes the turn. Use it to check on several turns at once, and use the wait tool when you would rather block until one ends."
STEER_CODEX_TURN_DESCRIPTION = "Adds new input to a Codex turn that is still running and that this toolset started, which changes paid Codex work in flight. Codex reads the new input while it keeps working on the same turn, so the turn can change course without starting over. A turn that already ended cannot be steered; continue its thread with a new turn instead. The result is the same handle, which you keep using to wait for the turn."
INTERRUPT_CODEX_TURN_DESCRIPTION = "Stops a running Codex turn that this toolset started, which changes paid Codex work in flight. Codex ends the turn as interrupted and keeps whatever it already produced, so the thread can be continued afterwards. Nothing is sent when the turn already ended. Collect the final snapshot of the interrupted turn with the wait tool."
TRACK_CODEX_THREAD_USAGE_DESCRIPTION = "Reports the token usage of every turn this host saw end on a Codex thread, and their estimated cost. Each turn is listed with its status and its own usage, and turns whose usage Codex never reported are marked as unavailable rather than counted as zero. The total adds up the turns, and the cost is marked incomplete when some usage or price is unknown. It reads only what this host recorded, so a thread this host never ran a turn on is refused."
LAUNCH_CODEX_SUBAGENTS_DESCRIPTION = "Starts several new Codex threads at once, one per prompt, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running after this call returns. Every thread gets the developer's settings, and the threads run side by side, independent of each other. The call returns one handle per prompt, in prompt order, as soon as Codex accepts each turn. If one start fails, every turn already started is interrupted and the error says how many there were."
START_CODEX_REVIEW_DESCRIPTION = "Starts a Codex code review of a thread's work, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running after this call returns. The target says what to review: the uncommitted changes, the difference from a base branch, one commit, or custom instructions. The review runs as a turn of its own and returns a handle as soon as Codex accepts it, and you collect its findings with the wait or result tool. The review is refused when the thread already has a running turn."
LIST_RECENT_CODEX_THREADS_DESCRIPTION = "Lists the most recently updated Codex threads of the account, newest first, without starting any work. It includes threads started from every kind of client, the threads that tools like these start included, so no source filter is needed. Each entry gives the thread id, its title, when it was created and last updated, the thread it was forked or spawned from when there is one, and its model. Use it to find recent work to read, continue or fork, and narrow it to one working folder when the account has many projects."
LIST_CODEX_THREADS_SINCE_DESCRIPTION = "Lists the Codex threads of the account that were updated at or after a moment in time, newest first, without starting any work. The moment is given in Unix seconds, and a window that holds no thread gives an empty list rather than an error. It includes threads started from every kind of client, the threads that tools like these start included. Each entry gives the thread id, title, timestamps, parent thread and model, and the list stops at its limit."
SEARCH_CODEX_THREADS_DESCRIPTION = "Finds the Codex threads of the account whose title matches a search text, newest first, without starting any work. Codex matches the text against each thread's title only, so search for words you expect in the title rather than in the conversation. It includes threads started from every kind of client, the threads that tools like these start included. Each entry gives the thread id, title, timestamps, parent thread and model, and the list stops at its limit."
GET_CODEX_THREAD_LINEAGE_DESCRIPTION = "Reads the chain of threads that a Codex thread came from, without starting any work. The first entry is the thread itself, and each next entry is the thread it was forked or spawned from, up to a thread with no parent. A parent that Codex no longer has ends the chain early, so the chain is complete only when its last entry has no parent. Use it to find the original thread of a fork or a subagent, or to see how a line of work branched."
LIST_CODEX_TURNS_DESCRIPTION = "Reads one page of the full transcript of a stored Codex thread, oldest turn first, without starting any work. Each turn gives its status, its final answer, any error message, its timestamps and every item Codex recorded for it. Any thread the account can see is accepted, including threads this host never ran. Transcripts can be long, so read a few turns at a time and pass the returned cursor to read the next page, which is empty when there are no more turns."
COMPACT_CODEX_THREAD_DESCRIPTION = "Starts Codex's compaction of a stored thread's history, which changes the stored thread and uses paid Codex model work to summarize it. The call returns as soon as Codex starts, and the compaction then runs in the background and finishes only while this host stays open. A thread with a running turn is refused, so wait for that turn to end first. A thread that this host has not loaded is loaded first, and it is refused while it has an unfinished goal, because loading it could let Codex continue that goal."
GET_CODEX_GOAL_DESCRIPTION = "Reads the goal stored on a Codex thread, without starting any work and without loading the thread. The result gives the goal's objective, its status, its token budget, the tokens and time it used so far, and when it was created and last updated. A thread that has no goal is reported as having none rather than as a failure. Use it to check on a goal run, or to see why the goal of a thread stopped."
RUN_CODEX_GOAL_DESCRIPTION = "Starts a goal run on a new Codex thread, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running across turns after this call returns. Codex keeps working toward the objective until it decides that the goal is complete, blocked or out of budget. The call returns a handle only after Codex has stored the goal, and a run whose goal did not take is stopped and reported as a failure. The sandbox, approvals, working folder and instructions come from the developer's settings, and you collect the outcome later with the wait or result tool."
LIST_CODEX_SKILLS_DESCRIPTION = "Lists the skills that Codex offers for one working folder, without starting any work. Each entry gives the skill's name, its description, the path of its instructions file, where the skill comes from and whether it is enabled. Skills can differ between folders, because a project can add skills of its own. Pass the exact name of an enabled skill to the run skill tool, which looks skills up in the working folder of the developer's settings."
RUN_CODEX_SKILL_DESCRIPTION = "Starts a new Codex thread whose first turn runs one Codex skill on a prompt, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running after this call returns. The skill is looked up by its exact name in the working folder of the developer's settings, and an unknown or disabled name is refused before any work starts. Codex receives the skill as part of the turn's input rather than as text in the prompt, so the turn follows the skill's own instructions. The call returns a handle only after Codex has recorded the skill in the turn's input, and you collect the outcome later with the wait or result tool."
RUN_CODEX_OPENAI_DOCS_DESCRIPTION = "Starts a new Codex thread that answers a question from the official OpenAI documentation, which begins paid Codex work that keeps running after this call returns. It runs the openai-docs skill that ships with Codex, which searches the documentation before it answers. The call is refused before any work starts when that skill is missing or disabled in the working folder of the developer's settings. It returns a handle only after Codex has recorded the skill in the turn's input, and you collect the answer later with the wait or result tool."

INCLUDE_HIDDEN_DESCRIPTION = "Whether the list also includes models that Codex hides from its own model picker. Hidden models are usually internal or special-purpose, so most callers leave them out. They are still accepted wherever a model is named, which is why a caller sometimes needs to see them. Leaving this out, or sending false, lists only the visible models."
SPAWN_PROMPT_DESCRIPTION = "The instruction for the first turn of the new thread, written as you would write it to a capable engineer. Codex reads it as the whole task for this turn, together with the developer's instructions for the thread. Send either this or a goal objective, never both, because a goal replaces the prompt. The text should say what done looks like, so the final answer can be checked."
GOAL_OBJECTIVE_DESCRIPTION = "The objective of a goal run, which Codex keeps working toward across as many turns as it needs. Codex decides on its own when the goal is complete, blocked or out of budget, and the run ends then. Send either this or a prompt, never both, because the objective is the run's whole instruction. Goal runs need a stored thread, so they are refused when the developer's settings make threads temporary."
SPAWN_GOAL_BUDGET_DESCRIPTION = "The number of tokens Codex may spend on a goal run before it stops the goal as out of budget. It applies only together with a goal objective and must be a whole number of at least one. Codex counts the budget itself, and a run stopped by it is reported as interrupted with a budget problem. Leave it out to let the goal run without a token budget of its own."
SPAWN_MODEL_DESCRIPTION = "The model for the new thread, which replaces the model in the developer's settings. It must be the id or name of a model the list models tool returns, because an unknown name is refused before any work starts. The thread keeps this model for later turns unless a turn names another. Leave it out to use the developer's model, or Codex's default when the developer set none."
CONTINUE_MODEL_DESCRIPTION = "The model for this one turn, which replaces the turn model in the developer's settings. It must be the id or name of a model the list models tool returns, because an unknown name is refused before any work starts. The thread's own model is unchanged for later turns. Leave it out to use the developer's turn model, or the thread's model when the developer set none."
EFFORT_DESCRIPTION = "The reasoning effort for the turn, which replaces the effort in the developer's settings. Higher effort lets the model think longer before it answers, which costs more tokens and time. The value must be one of the reasoning efforts Codex accepts, and the list models tool shows which ones each model supports. Leave it out to use the developer's effort or the model's default."
THREAD_NAME_DESCRIPTION = "A short human-readable name for the new thread, set before its first turn starts. The name shows up when threads are listed, so it helps you and other agents find the thread again later. It does not change how the thread runs. Leave it out to use the developer's name, or to leave the thread unnamed when the developer set none."
MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS_DESCRIPTION = "The most output tokens this turn may produce before Vidbyte stops it, as a whole number of at least one. Codex has no output limit of its own, so Vidbyte watches the turn's usage and sends one interrupt once the turn's own output passes the cap. A turn stopped this way is reported as interrupted with a budget problem, and it keeps what it already produced. Leave it out to use the developer's cap, or no cap when the developer set none."
TURN_TIMEOUT_DESCRIPTION = "The most seconds this turn may run before Vidbyte stops it, as a number greater than zero. When the time passes, Vidbyte sends the turn one interrupt, and a turn stopped this way is reported as interrupted with a timeout problem. The limit applies to the running turn, not to this call, which returns as soon as Codex accepts the work. Leave it out to use the developer's limit, or no limit when the developer set none."
OUTPUT_SCHEMA_DESCRIPTION = "A JSON Schema object that describes the shape of the turn's final answer. Codex is asked to answer in that shape, because the schema is sent to Codex with the turn. When the turn completes, the answer is checked only for being valid JSON and is not compared with the schema. An answer that is not valid JSON is reported with a schema problem, and its raw text is still returned. Leave it out to accept a free-form answer, or to use the developer's schema when one was set."
THREAD_ID_DESCRIPTION = "The id of a stored Codex thread, as returned in a handle or by the list or read tools. Any thread the Codex account can see is accepted, including threads started elsewhere. An unknown id is refused with a problem that says how to find the right thread. Copy the id exactly, because thread ids are opaque and case sensitive."
CONTINUE_PROMPT_DESCRIPTION = "The instruction for the new turn on the existing thread. Codex reads it together with the thread's whole history, so it can refer to earlier work without repeating it. The text should say what done looks like for this turn, so the final answer can be checked. It must not be empty."
FROM_TURN_ID_DESCRIPTION = "The id of the completed turn the fork goes through, inclusive. The new thread holds every turn up to and including this one, and none after it. The turn must be completed, because a running, failed or unknown turn cannot be forked through. Leave it out to fork through the last completed turn of the thread."
EXCLUDE_TURNS_DESCRIPTION = "Whether the result leaves out the list of turns of the new thread. It only shapes this answer: the forked thread still holds every turn up to the fork point. Use it to keep the answer short when the thread has a long history. Leaving it out, or sending false, includes the turns."
CWD_DESCRIPTION = "A working folder that the listed threads must have been started in. Only threads whose working folder matches exactly are listed. Use it to find the threads of one project when the account has many. Leave it out to list threads from every folder."
SEARCH_DESCRIPTION = "Text that the listed threads' names or previews must contain. Codex matches it against the stored thread text and lists only the matches. Use it to find a thread when you remember part of its task but not its id. Leave it out to list threads without a text filter."
ARCHIVED_DESCRIPTION = "Whether to list archived threads instead of active ones. Archived threads are kept by Codex but hidden from the usual list. Use it to find older work that was put away. Leaving it out, or sending false, lists the threads that are not archived."
SOURCE_KINDS_DESCRIPTION = "The kinds of client that started the threads to list, as a list of Codex source kind values. Without this filter Codex lists only threads started interactively, so threads started through the app server, which is how these tools start them, need that source here. Several kinds can be combined in one list. Leave it out to use Codex's default interactive sources."
SORT_DESCRIPTION = "The thread field the list is sorted by, as one of the Codex thread sort key values. Sorting by creation time or update time is the usual choice when looking for recent work. The sort applies across pages, so keep it the same while paging with the cursor. Leave it out to use Codex's default order."
SORT_DIRECTION_DESCRIPTION = "Whether the sorted list runs in ascending or descending order. Descending order puts the newest or highest values first, and ascending order puts them last. It applies to the sort field chosen for the list. Leave it out to use Codex's default direction."
CURSOR_DESCRIPTION = "The cursor that a previous list call returned, which selects the next page. Pass it unchanged together with the same filters and sort as the call that returned it. An empty or missing cursor reads the first page. When a page comes back with an empty cursor, there are no more pages."
INCLUDE_TURNS_DESCRIPTION = "Whether the result also summarizes each turn of the thread. A summary gives each turn's status, final answer, error message and timestamps. Long threads make the answer long, so ask for turns only when you need them. Leaving it out, or sending false, returns the thread without its turns."
TURN_ID_DESCRIPTION = "The id of a turn, as returned in the handle of the call that started it. Together with the thread id it names exactly one turn, and only turns that this toolset started are accepted. A handle from anywhere else is refused with a problem. Copy the id exactly, because turn ids are opaque and case sensitive."
WAIT_TIMEOUT_DESCRIPTION = "The most seconds to wait for the turn to end, as a number greater than zero. The wait never runs longer than a few minutes per call, so larger values are cut down to that cap. If the turn is still running when the time is up, the result says so and the turn keeps running. Leave it out to wait up to the cap."
STEER_PROMPT_DESCRIPTION = "The new input to add to the running turn. Codex reads it while it keeps working, so it can change direction or add a detail without starting over. Keep it focused on what should change, because the turn already has its original task. It must not be empty."
PROMPTS_DESCRIPTION = "The instructions for the new threads, one string per thread, in the order the handles come back. Each prompt is the whole task of its own thread, and the threads cannot see each other's work. Every prompt starts paid work, so send only as many as the work needs. The list must hold at least one prompt."
TARGET_KIND_DESCRIPTION = "What the review looks at, as one of the Codex review target values. It can be the uncommitted changes in the working folder, the difference from a base branch, one commit, or custom instructions. The other review fields fill in the target: a branch for a base branch, a commit id and title for a commit, and instructions for a custom review. Choose the narrowest target that covers the work to review."
BRANCH_DESCRIPTION = "The base branch the changes are compared with when the review target is a base branch. The review covers every change between that branch and the current work. The other review targets refuse it. Leave it out when the target is not a base branch."
SHA_DESCRIPTION = "The id of the commit to review when the review target is one commit. The review covers the changes that commit made. The other review targets refuse it. Leave it out when the target is not a commit."
TITLE_DESCRIPTION = "A short title for the commit under review, shown with the review. It helps the review describe the commit it looked at. The other review targets refuse it. Leave it out when the commit needs no title."
INSTRUCTIONS_DESCRIPTION = "The instructions of a custom review, which tell Codex what to look at and what to look for. The other review targets refuse them. Write them as you would brief a careful reviewer, saying what matters most. Leave them out when the target is not custom."
DELIVERY_DESCRIPTION = "Where Codex runs the review: inline on the reviewed thread, or detached on a new thread of its own. An inline review becomes part of the thread's history, while a detached one leaves the thread untouched. Codex has deprecated the detached mode, so prefer inline unless the thread must stay unchanged. Leave it out to review inline."
LIMIT_DESCRIPTION = "The most threads to list, as a whole number of at least one. The list reads as many pages from Codex as it needs to reach this number, and it stops early when there are no more threads. Very large values are refused, so ask for only as many threads as you need. Leave it out to list a small default number of threads."
SINCE_DESCRIPTION = "The earliest update time of the threads to list, in Unix seconds, as a whole number of at least zero. Only threads updated at or after this moment are listed, newest first. A moment after the last update of every thread gives an empty list. Use the recent threads tool instead when any update time will do."
QUERY_DESCRIPTION = "The text to look for in the thread titles, which must not be empty. Codex matches it against each thread's title, so a thread whose title does not match is left out even when its conversation mentions the text. Use words you expect in the title of the thread you are looking for. The search applies together with the working folder, when one is given."
TURN_CURSOR_DESCRIPTION = "The cursor that a previous call for the same thread returned, which selects the next page of turns. Pass it unchanged to continue reading the transcript where the last page ended. An empty or missing cursor reads from the first turn of the thread. When a page comes back with an empty cursor, there are no more turns."
TURN_LIMIT_DESCRIPTION = "The most turns to return in this page, as a whole number of at least one. Pages come oldest first and are bounded in turns, not in size, so a few long turns can still make a large answer. Leave it out to get a small default page. Page through the transcript with the cursor instead of asking for every turn at once."
RUN_GOAL_OBJECTIVE_DESCRIPTION = "The objective of the goal run, which Codex keeps working toward across as many turns as it needs. Write it as you would brief a capable engineer and say what done looks like, because Codex decides on its own when the goal is complete. It is the whole instruction of the run, so it must not be empty. The objective is stored with the thread, where the get goal tool can read it back."
RUN_GOAL_BUDGET_DESCRIPTION = "The number of tokens Codex may spend on the goal run before it stops the goal as out of budget, as a whole number of at least one. Codex counts the budget itself, across every turn of the run. A run stopped by its budget keeps what it already produced. Leave it out to let the goal run without a token budget of its own."
SKILLS_CWD_DESCRIPTION = "The working folder whose skills are listed. Codex gathers skills from the user's own folder, from the project at that folder, from the system skills it ships with and from skills an administrator set up, so the list can differ between folders. Leave it out to list the skills for the Codex runtime's own working folder. The run skill tool looks skills up in the working folder of the developer's settings, which may differ from this one."
SKILL_NAME_DESCRIPTION = "The exact name of the Codex skill to run, as the list skills tool reports it. The name must match exactly, and only an enabled skill can run. An unknown name is refused with the names of the enabled skills, and a disabled one is refused with a note that it is disabled. Neither refusal starts any work."
SKILL_PROMPT_DESCRIPTION = "The instruction for the first turn of the skill run, written as you would write it to a capable engineer. Codex reads it together with the skill's own instructions, so say what you need from the skill rather than how the skill works. The text should say what done looks like, so the final answer can be checked. It must not be empty."
QUESTION_DESCRIPTION = "The question to answer from the official OpenAI documentation. Ask it the way you would ask an expert on the OpenAI platform, and name the product, interface or feature it is about. Codex searches the documentation for the answer, so a precise question gets a precise answer. It must not be empty."
